  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Tournament Poster",
    "overview": "The app is the tournament's poster. Every screen leads with one huge condensed statement in Anton, uppercase: what you have to do (\"Ciao Marco, hai 10 cose da fare\"), who you play (\"Por Tres contro Le Bandejas\"), where you stand (a 72px \"2°\"). Everything else steps back to a quiet supporting line in Atkinson Hyperlegible Next. The category default, an even list of identical rows with outline buttons, is what this world replaces: content is ranked into blocks, and the block's fill says what kind of thing it is.\n\nThe material is printed matter. A warm paper ground, ink-black text, solid 2px ink borders on every block and control, flat colour fills with no gradients or textures. Colour has jobs, not moods: ball lime is the one thing to do now (the first feed block, the primary action, the current selection, the active nav item, the highlighter on the key word of a headline); clay coral is availability and time windows; paper is everything else. The navigation bar is ink black in both themes. Dark is the poster in negative: a near-black ground with paper text and paper borders, the \"next match\" block flips to a paper fill with black ink, and the lime and clay fills stay identical with black ink.\n\nThe audience is mixed-age amateur players (many 50+) on phones in the sun, a court manager at the front desk on a phone or tablet, and a volunteer organizer at a desktop. So the poster is never at the expense of reading: body text is 17px, every target is at least 44px, nothing is carried by colour alone, and the state colours (ok, warn, danger, info, idle) stay with an icon and an Italian word.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "One poster statement per screen, Anton uppercase; everything else is Atkinson at reading size.",
      "Paper ground, ink text, 2px ink borders on blocks and controls; flat fills, no texture.",
      "Lime = the one thing to do now; clay = availability; paper = everything else; ink fill = your next match and \"today\".",
      "The black bar (tab bar, sidebar) with lime marking where you are.",
      "Dark theme is the same poster in negative; lime and clay never change.",
      "State is always tone + icon + text (+ pattern in the court lanes)."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Lime Rule",
        "body": "Lime marks the single thing to do now. One lime block at the top of the feed, one primary button per screen, one highlighted word per headline. A second lime element on the same screen is either the selection or the active nav item, never a second \"most important\" thing.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Fills Don't Invert Rule",
        "body": "Lime and clay are the same in both themes and always carry `edge` borders, `edge` buttons and black ink. Only paper and ink swap in dark.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Never By Colour Rule",
        "body": "Every state colour, zone and deadline comes with an icon and a word; in the court lanes also with a pattern. If you removed the colour, the screen must still say the same thing.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Statement Rule",
        "body": "Each screen has one poster statement at the top (the h1) and poster section titles under it; inside the content only the lime block's big line speaks at Poster SM, the other blocks step down to Poster Block. If two things on a screen are equally huge, neither is the statement.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Anton Is Never Bold Rule",
        "body": "Anton has one weight (400). Never apply `font-bold` to poster type, and never set running text, sentences or buttons in Anton. Connecting words inside a poster line (\"contro\") drop to Atkinson, lowercase.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Highlighter Rule",
        "body": "Emphasis inside a headline is a lime `.mark` behind one word or numeral (inline-block, ink text), not a colour change of the text.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Flat Poster Rule",
        "body": "Nothing printed on the poster floats. To stand out, a block gets a fill or a bigger statement, never a shadow.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** open every screen with one Anton uppercase statement (Poster on phones, Poster XL from 640px) and put everything else at reading size.",
      "**Do** use lime for exactly one \"do this now\" thing per screen, plus the selection and the active nav item.",
      "**Do** use clay for availability and time windows, and only there.",
      "**Do** give every paper block and control a 2px ink border, and every lime or clay fill a 2px `edge` border with `ink` buttons inside.",
      "**Do** collapse repeated items of the same kind into one group block with the list inside.",
      "**Do** pair every state colour with an icon and an Italian word, and in the lanes with a pattern (hatch occupied, cross closure, dots few free).",
      "**Do** put tabular numerals on every time, date, score, count and position.",
      "**Do** keep every target at least 44px and running text at least 16px (body 17px).",
      "**Do** draw anything that lives on a court and a time as `CourtLanes`, never as a uniform card grid.",
      "**Do** keep the navigation in the black bar in both themes, with lime for the active item."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** put a small kicker or eyebrow label above a heading; the poster h1 stands alone (a back link is not a kicker).",
      "**Don't** fake-bold Anton or set sentences, buttons or body copy in it.",
      "**Don't** add drop shadows to blocks on the page; shadows are for sheets, dialogs, menus and sticky bars.",
      "**Don't** introduce gradients, textures, glass or gradient text; fills are flat printed inks (state hatching is a pattern, not a material).",
      "**Don't** use coloured side stripes to tag blocks; a block's meaning comes from its full fill.",
      "**Don't** invert lime or clay in dark, or put paper text on them.",
      "**Don't** rely on colour alone for any state, zone or deadline.",
      "**Don't** make two blocks on one screen speak at the same poster size."
    ]
  }
}
